# Input Event Service Implementation Plan

Source proposal: `Docs/input-event-service-proposal.md`

## Goal

Implement contract major 1 of the namespace-bound `InputEventServiceProvider`
and its process-wide shared input runtime. The implementation must centralize
native/Lua input interception, synchronous input snapshots, continuous pointer
sampling, private Core arbitration, and public `MAP_CLICK`/`RAW_CLICK`
interception and observation without moving tooltip, context-menu, or UserPrompt
policy into the generic service.

The common `MAP_CLICK` contract means that a host-classified mouse input occurred
over an exact map tile and complete generic UI resolution proved that no UI
element obstructed it. `RAW_CLICK` means only that a host-classified mouse input
occurred while DFHack supplied an exact map coordinate. Both event types apply to
all host-classified mouse keys, including button, press/release, wheel, and future
mouse boundaries.

## Assumptions and fixed boundaries

- [ ] Treat `Docs/input-event-service-proposal.md` as authoritative when this
  plan and implementation details differ.
- [ ] Keep this implementation inside DwarfUICore; do not migrate external
  DwarfUI consumers as part of this plan.
- [ ] Preserve tooltip, context-menu, and UserPrompt target selection,
  presentation, state transitions, callbacks, and service-specific consumption
  semantics.
- [ ] Reuse the existing validated immutable `ScreenPosition` and
  `MapTilePosition` public value types.
- [ ] Use immutable numeric enum tables for every closed discriminator set.
- [ ] Treat hook support and UI-resolution support as separate capabilities:
  raw events require a hook-supported input surface, while semantic map events
  additionally require complete UI-resolution support.
- [ ] Treat the original intercepted keys table as read-only in every private
  Core consumer.
- [ ] Do not add public keyboard events, synthesized hover/drag/double-click
  gestures, priorities, cancellation, event transformation, or a generic string
  event bus.

## Phase 1: Contract types and canonical snapshot factory

Outcome: Shared immutable input facts and discriminator types exist independently
of the current context-menu owner, without changing active input behavior.

- [x] Define the immutable numeric private `InputDispatchResult` enum with
  `PASS` and `CONSUME` members.
- [x] Define immutable numeric public enum tables for `InputEventType` and
  `InputEventDisposition` with the approved version-1 members.
  - [x] Expose `MAP_CLICK` and `RAW_CLICK` through `InputEventType`.
  - [x] Expose `PASS` and `CONSUME` through `InputEventDisposition`.
  - [x] Construct the tables with DwarfUICore's immutable-enum helper.
- [x] Define immutable copied `MouseInput` values containing one exact
  host-provided mouse key.
- [x] Implement one canonical snapshot factory for intercepted input snapshots
  and continuous pointer samples.
  - [x] Allocate process-wide monotonic sequence values shared by both sample
    paths.
  - [x] Copy and validate optional `ScreenPosition` and `MapTilePosition`
    values.
  - [x] Collect every host-classified mouse key in an intercepted keys table.
  - [x] Sort copied `MouseInput` values deterministically by key.
  - [x] Produce immutable `InputSnapshot` values with `sequence`,
    `mouse_inputs`, `screen_position`, and `map_position`.
  - [x] Produce immutable `PointerSample` values with `sequence`,
    `screen_position`, and `map_position`.
  - [x] Never store parallel per-axis coordinate fields or a redundant
    coordinate-space discriminator.
- [x] Keep map-coordinate acquisition demand-driven and sample each requested
  coordinate source at most once per snapshot.
- [x] Make synchronous input-snapshot demand explicit and independently
  countable for screen coordinates, map coordinates, and UI-root resolution so
  each later private consumer and public subscription can contribute only the
  facts its contract requires.
- [x] Add LuaDoc declarations for every new class, method, enum, alias, and
  public field.
- [x] Add focused unit coverage for copied positions, immutability, sorted mouse
  keys, empty non-mouse collections, malformed/nil positions, shared sequencing,
  and demand-aware sampling.

Completion criteria:

- [x] One canonical factory can create both immutable sample shapes with no
  consumer-specific policy.
- [x] Each intercepted input table produces at most one screen sample and one
  map sample, according to aggregate demand.
- [x] Snapshot/value tests prove that coordinates exist only inside immutable
  position values and that all host-classified mouse keys are retained.

## Phase 2: Relocate and preserve the process input hook

Outcome: Input Event owns the existing process-wide native and Lua interception
chain while current consumers continue to behave through a temporary internal
compatibility boundary.

- [x] Relocate the generic responsibilities of
  `dwarfuicore/context_menu/input_hook.lua` beneath the Input Event runtime.
- [x] Preserve native overlay and Lua-screen input ownership behavior.
  - [x] Preserve compatible predecessor and foreign-wrapper chains.
  - [x] Prevent duplicate active trampolines.
  - [x] Preserve current module/method replacement repair behavior.
  - [x] Keep trampolines inert when no current dispatcher is installed.
  - [x] Prevent retired runtime generations from dispatching into current
    state.
  - [x] Restore only wrappers still owned by DwarfUICore during destructive
    shutdown.
- [x] Keep `dwarfuicore/context_menu/input_hook.lua` as a temporary private
  compatibility export where required by unmigrated Core consumers.
- [x] Ensure no public API or external consumer depends on the compatibility
  export.
- [x] Activate the input hook only while a private intercepted-input consumer or
  public Input Event subscription has demand.
- [x] Make removal of the final demand source leave the input path inert while
  permitting reload-adoptable trampolines to remain installed.
- [x] Add focused regression coverage for native/Lua interception, predecessor
  delegation, foreign wrappers, duplicate prevention, repair, inert dispatch,
  owned cleanup, and generation retirement/adoption.

Completion criteria:

- [x] Exactly one process-wide native/Lua input interception chain is active.
- [x] Existing context-menu input behavior remains unchanged through the
  temporary compatibility boundary.
- [x] Hook-chain regressions pass before any private consumer is migrated.

## Phase 3: Establish private Core arbitration and migrate context menu

Outcome: The shared runtime creates one pre-arbitration input snapshot and
context menu consumes it through deterministic private arbitration without
resampling coordinates.

- [x] Add the typed private `CoreInputConsumer(keys, snapshot)` collaboration
  boundary.
- [x] Create exactly one immutable `InputSnapshot` before private arbitration
  for every intercepted input table.
- [x] Pass the same unchanged original keys table and same snapshot to every
  eligible private consumer.
- [x] Enforce the private-consumer contract that the original keys table is
  read-only and must not be mutated.
- [x] Assemble private consumers in a fixed Core-defined order rather than by
  registration timing or caller-selected priority.
- [x] Stop private arbitration on the first `InputDispatchResult.CONSUME`.
  - [x] Suppress both public event channels for every Core-consumed input table.
  - [x] Skip inherited input handling.
  - [x] Return `true` from the input trampoline.
- [x] Delegate the original keys table unchanged when all private consumers
  return `PASS`.
- [x] Prevent duplicate private-consumer attachment and public access to private
  registration or priority controls.
- [x] Preserve consumer-specific fail-closed ownership rules without inferring
  ownership from an exception alone.
- [x] Require an ownership-sensitive private consumer to contain failures that
  occur after it classifies an input table as owned and return `CONSUME` when its
  contract requires fail-closed behavior.
- [x] Record an exception that escapes a private consumer and treat it as `PASS`
  because the dispatcher received no explicit ownership result; do not infer
  consumption from the exception itself.
- [x] Migrate context menu to shared snapshots and private arbitration.
  - [x] Register and remove context menu's synchronous screen/map snapshot demand
    with its private-consumer lifecycle.
  - [x] Remove context menu's direct ownership of generic sampling.
  - [x] Prohibit context menu from resampling screen or map coordinates for the
    same input table.
  - [x] Retain its widget/map contribution registries and target detection.
  - [x] Retain opening eligibility, menu assembly, selection callbacks, menu
    screen ownership, and menu-specific consumption.
- [x] Until UserPrompt is migrated in Phase 4, preserve its existing
  compatibility-path precedence over the newly migrated context-menu consumer.
- [x] Add focused tests for deterministic private ordering, first-consumer
  consumption, pass-through delegation, read-only keys expectations, snapshot
  identity, suppression, failure handling, and unchanged context-menu behavior.
- [x] Add a transitional regression proving an active compatibility-path
  UserPrompt still receives input before the migrated context-menu consumer.

Completion criteria:

- [x] Context menu runs through Input Event private arbitration with no generic
  coordinate resampling.
- [x] Private consumption suppresses future public dispatch and inherited
  handling for every consumed input table, not only left releases.
- [x] Context-menu behavioral regressions pass before UserPrompt is migrated.

## Phase 4: Migrate UserPrompt with prompt-first ownership

Outcome: UserPrompt uses the same snapshot/arbitration boundary while preserving
its authoritative precedence and exclusive click ownership.

- [x] Attach UserPrompt as the highest-precedence private Core input consumer
  while a prompt is active.
- [x] Register synchronous input and map-snapshot demand while a prompt is active
  and remove both contributions when the prompt becomes inactive.
- [x] Preserve UserPrompt completion, cancellation, consumption, indicator,
  presentation, callback, and failure contracts.
- [x] Supply the shared pre-arbitration snapshot to completion and cancellation
  logic.
- [x] Prohibit UserPrompt from resampling screen or map coordinates for the same
  input table.
- [x] Suppress both public event channels for a prompt-consumed left release.
- [x] Ensure inactive UserPrompt contributes no input demand and cannot affect
  public dispatch or context-menu behavior.
- [x] Add focused tests for prompt-first ordering, active/inactive demand,
  completion/cancellation coordinates, consumed-click suppression, context-menu
  exclusion, callback behavior, and no resampling.

Completion criteria:

- [x] UserPrompt and context menu receive the same pre-arbitration facts in the
  required fixed order.
- [x] Prompt-owned input cannot leak to public interceptors, observers,
  inherited handling, or context-menu opening.
- [x] Existing UserPrompt and context-menu regression suites remain green.

## Phase 5: Move pointer-poller ownership and migrate tooltip

Outcome: Input Event owns one process-wide demand-driven pointer poller, and
tooltip consumes shared pointer samples without depending on public click
subscriptions.

- [x] Move `dwarfuicore/pointer_poller.lua` ownership and demand accounting into
  the Input Event runtime.
- [x] Maintain separate demand counts for intercepted input, screen-pointer
  polling, and map-pointer polling.
- [x] Activate continuous polling only while a private continuous-pointer
  consumer exists.
- [x] Request map coordinates only while a current continuous consumer explicitly
  requires them.
- [x] Ensure widget-only tooltip targets request screen samples without calling
  `dfhack.gui.getMousePos()` every frame.
- [x] Ensure exact map-tooltip targets create map-coordinate demand.
- [x] Migrate tooltip polling predicates into private subscriptions or demand
  contributions.
- [x] Preserve tooltip widget/map registrations, target detection, pointer
  enter/update/leave mediation, immutable presentation intent, and
  completed-render ownership.
- [x] Keep tooltip independent of public map-click/raw-click callbacks and public
  observer registration.
- [x] Invalidate current sampled facts on world unload, root loss, and surface
  replacement without removing process-owned public subscriptions.
- [x] Add focused tests for demand transitions, pointer-only/map polling,
  sequence sharing, sample invalidation, teardown, and unchanged tooltip
  behavior.

Completion criteria:

- [x] Exactly one process-wide pointer poller remains.
- [x] Map polling never occurs without current map demand.
- [x] Tooltip regressions prove that polling ownership moved without changing
  target or presentation semantics.

## Phase 6: Build the process runtime and namespace-bound public provider

Outcome: Consumers can acquire immutable contract-major-1 API objects and manage
typed namespace-bound subscriptions without yet depending on unfinished event
semantics.

- [x] Implement one lazy, transactional, process-wide Input Event runtime.
- [x] Export `InputEventServiceProvider` through
  `reqscript('dwarfuicore/services')` using the existing exact-version provider
  pattern.
- [x] Return a distinct immutable `InputEventServiceApi` for every `new()` call
  while sharing one namespace domain for APIs bound to the same namespace.
- [x] Expose immutable `EventType` and `Disposition` enum tables on each API.
- [x] Implement the generic public methods only:
  - [x] `observe(event_type, callback)`.
  - [x] `intercept(event_type, handler)`.
  - [x] `unsubscribe(handle)`.
  - [x] `is_subscribed(handle)`.
  - [x] `clear_namespace()`.
- [x] Validate callback/handler type and exact numeric enum membership before
  runtime initialization or registry mutation.
- [x] Reject strings and unknown numbers while accepting a valid numeric member
  regardless of its source.
- [x] Create a distinct opaque subscription for each successful registration,
  bound to service kind, contract major, namespace, generation, local identity,
  event type, and dispatch channel.
- [x] Wire every active public subscription into synchronous input-snapshot
  demand accounting.
  - [x] Both event types contribute input-hook and map-coordinate demand.
  - [x] `MAP_CLICK` additionally contributes screen-coordinate and UI-root
    resolution demand.
  - [x] Unsubscription, namespace cleanup, generation retirement, and failed
    registration remove or avoid publishing the corresponding contributions.
  - [x] Removing the final contribution makes each affected runtime path inert.
- [x] Permit multiple registrations from one namespace and retain global
  registration sequence.
- [x] Strongly retain callbacks while subscriptions are active; do not make API
  or handle garbage collection an implicit unsubscribe operation.
- [x] Implement same-domain inactive, malformed, stale-generation, and
  foreign-domain handle behavior with established error precedence.
- [x] Ensure namespace cleanup cannot remove private Core consumers or another
  namespace's subscriptions.
- [x] Add the modules to `dwarfuicore/module_registry.lua` in dependency order
  and update registry, service-export, provider, and package declaration tests.
- [x] Add focused tests for acquisition identity, immutability, validation
  ordering, namespaces, handles, cleanup, stale APIs/handles, foreign handles,
  repeated construction, and transactional initialization failure.

Completion criteria:

- [x] The public surface contains one generic `observe` method and one generic
  `intercept` method selected by immutable numeric event type.
- [x] Namespace, identity, generation, service-kind, and contract-major
  boundaries are enforced by tests.
- [x] Repeated acquisition reuses one healthy process runtime without replacing
  a healthy hook chain.

## Phase 7: Derive raw and unobstructed map events

Outcome: The runtime creates immutable event values with the exact approved raw
and semantic eligibility guarantees for every host-classified mouse boundary.

- [x] Derive at most one `RawClickEvent` per qualifying intercepted input table
  after private arbitration.
  - [x] Require a hook-supported current input surface.
  - [x] Require at least one host-classified mouse key.
  - [x] Require that no private Core consumer consumed the table.
  - [x] Require one valid non-nil map position from the shared snapshot.
  - [x] Include the complete immutable sorted `mouse_inputs` collection,
    sequence, map position, and optional screen position.
- [x] Treat every host-classified mouse key as eligible without requiring paired
  boundaries, a gesture latch, or a preceding down event.
- [x] Do not synthesize double-clicks or combine multiple keys tables into one
  gesture.
- [x] Suppress both public channels when the map position is nil or malformed.
- [x] Permit a raw event with nil screen position when its map position is valid.
- [x] Implement generic front-to-back UI obstruction resolution for semantic
  map events.
  - [x] Resolve the native viewscreen widget root, current Lua screen, active
    overlay roots, and every additional shared Core pointer root.
  - [x] Resolve duplicate roots once in effective front-to-back order.
  - [x] Preserve generic visibility, activity, clipping, frame, child-order, and
    `PointerPolicy` meanings.
  - [x] Treat `MISS`, `PASS`, and `NONE` as non-obstructing and continue to lower
    roots.
  - [x] Treat `TARGET` and `BLOCKED` as obstructing.
  - [x] Treat unavailable screen coordinates, uninspectable roots, unresolved
    order, and unsupported UI-resolution surfaces as unable to prove
    unobstructed ownership.
- [x] Keep hook support distinct from UI-resolution support so an unresolved UI
  surface can still publish an otherwise eligible raw event.
- [x] Perform root resolution only when the captured dispatch state contains an
  eligible semantic subscription or explicit private UI-resolution demand.
- [x] Derive at most one immutable `MapClickedEvent` when all raw conditions hold
  and complete root resolution proves the sampled screen position unobstructed.
- [x] Give paired raw and semantic events the same sequence, copied positions,
  and mouse-input collection.
- [x] Resolve eligibility against pre-delegation coordinates and UI state without
  reclassification after inherited handling.
- [x] Add focused matrix coverage for every mouse-key class, mixed key tables,
  off-map input, nil screen coordinates, each pointer policy, overlapping roots,
  duplicate roots, unknown/unsupported roots, UI obstruction, semantic demand,
  and paired event identity.

Completion criteria:

- [x] Raw-event tests prove coordinate qualification without any unobstructed-map
  claim.
- [x] Semantic-event tests prove complete unobstructed UI resolution and
  conservative suppression when that proof is unavailable.
- [x] Press, release, wheel, and additional currently host-supported mouse keys
  use the same per-input-table contract; future keys remain eligible by virtue
  of host classification without requiring tests for nonexistent events.

## Phase 8: Implement deterministic public interception and observation

Outcome: Eligible semantic and raw subscriptions execute through the approved
pre-delegation interception and post-delegation observation channels with
deterministic mutation, recursion, consumption, and error behavior.

- [x] Before invoking the first public callback for an input event, capture both
  stable interceptor and observer candidate snapshots.
- [x] Interleave eligible semantic and raw subscriptions by global registration
  sequence within each channel.
- [x] Exclude subscriptions added during the current event from both captured
  snapshots.
- [x] Check active state immediately before each callback and skip subscriptions
  removed before their turn.
- [x] Dispatch interceptors after private arbitration and before inherited input
  handling.
- [x] Continue on exact `Disposition.PASS`.
- [x] On the first exact `Disposition.CONSUME`, stop later interceptors, skip
  inherited handling, and make the trampoline return `true`.
- [x] Treat nil, booleans, unknown numbers, exceptions, and every other invalid
  interceptor return as a recorded failure and fail open as `PASS`.
- [x] Delegate the original keys table unchanged and preserve the inherited
  return value when every interceptor passes.
- [x] Dispatch observers after inherited handling or after public consumption
  skips delegation.
- [x] Keep observers eligible when an interceptor consumes or inherited handling
  reports that it handled the input.
- [x] Ignore every observer return value and prevent observers from altering
  consumption or inherited results.
- [x] Deliver one shared immutable event object to callbacks for the same event
  type and channel.
- [x] Retain public event values only for dispatch unless a consumer explicitly
  retains them.
- [x] Contain and record callback failures against the responsible subscription
  and namespace without suppressing later observers or disabling Core services.
- [x] Give recursive input dispatch a later sequence and independent interceptor
  and observer snapshots captured at the same defined points.
- [x] Add focused tests for cross-event ordering, first-consume behavior,
  pass-through, observer timing, inherited results, callback mutation,
  unsubscription-before-turn, registration-during-dispatch, recursion, invalid
  returns, exceptions, and failure isolation.

Completion criteria:

- [x] `intercept()` is observably pre-delegation and is the only public operation
  capable of consuming input.
- [x] `observe()` is observably post-delegation and cannot affect ownership or
  inherited results.
- [x] Ordering, mutation, recursive dispatch, and failure tests establish one
  deterministic contract across both event types.

## Phase 9: Complete lifecycle, reload, diagnostics, and error contracts

Outcome: The service remains healthy and isolated across demand transitions,
failures, surface changes, shutdown, and explicit DwarfUICore reload.

- [x] Use established exception transport, stable token categories, and
  precedence for provider construction and API calls.
- [x] Use the exact public error prefixes documented by the proposal.
- [x] Record observer and interceptor failures as private asynchronous dispatch
  failures rather than synchronous API-call failures.
- [x] Provide private diagnostics for counts, identities, demand, sequences,
  surfaces, and failure text without exposing another namespace's callback.
- [x] Preserve subscriptions across world unload, root loss, and surface
  replacement while invalidating sampled facts.
- [x] Retire old dispatchers before publishing new-generation state.
- [x] Permit adoption of compatible surviving hook trampolines by the next
  runtime generation.
- [x] Invalidate APIs and handles from the retired generation.
- [x] Ensure partial initialization publishes neither a healthy facade nor a
  subscription.
- [x] Ensure failures in public callbacks cannot disable private Core consumers.
- [x] Ensure failures in tooltip, context menu, or UserPrompt cannot remove
  unrelated public subscriptions.
- [x] Restore only still-owned wrappers during destructive shutdown.
- [x] Add focused tests for lazy construction, failure rollback, demand teardown,
  world/root/surface invalidation, diagnostics privacy, reload adoption,
  generation retirement, stale handles, callback isolation, and owned cleanup.

Completion criteria:

- [x] Reload leaves one healthy runtime, one hook chain, and one pointer poller.
- [x] Retired state is inert and stale APIs/handles are rejected consistently.
- [x] Failure injection proves namespace and service isolation without weakening
  base-game input behavior.

## Phase 10: Remove compatibility ownership and finalize integration

Outcome: Every Core consumer uses the shared owner directly and no duplicate or
context-menu-named generic input infrastructure remains.

- [ ] Confirm context menu, UserPrompt, and tooltip no longer import the
  temporary context-menu compatibility input owner.
- [ ] Remove the temporary `dwarfuicore/context_menu/input_hook.lua`
  compatibility export after all internal imports are migrated.
- [ ] Remove or consolidate superseded context-menu sampler and pointer-poller
  ownership without deleting consumer-specific target policy.
- [ ] Confirm public click dispatch is not used as the private communication
  mechanism for tooltip, context menu, or UserPrompt.
- [ ] Update module registry order, reload ownership, package manifests, source
  declarations, and package-content expectations for the final module layout.
- [ ] Document the public provider, exact event meanings, all-mouse-key scope,
  generic `observe`/`intercept` usage, dispositions, handles, callback timing,
  consumption, immutability, and error behavior.
- [ ] Keep public documentation free of unsupported future features and make the
  raw event's lack of UI-obstruction guarantee explicit.
- [ ] Add regressions proving no duplicate process hook or poller chain remains
  and no removed compatibility path is packaged.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] Generic input interception, sampling, and polling have one Input Event
  owner.
- [ ] Consumer-specific target, state, presentation, and ownership policy remain
  in their original services.
- [ ] The published source and package contain no obsolete compatibility owner.

## Phase 11: Qualification and evidence

Outcome: Distinct source, unit, integration, package, reload, and live-runtime
evidence demonstrate the complete proposal contract without conflating proof
boundaries.

- [ ] Run focused unit suites for snapshot/value behavior, hook ownership,
  arbitration, all consumer migrations, provider/handles, event derivation,
  dispatch ordering, demand, failures, and reload.
- [ ] Run existing tooltip, context-menu, UserPrompt, provider, registry, and
  package regression suites.
- [ ] Run the complete DwarfUICore unit suite.
- [ ] Run Lua syntax/source-declaration validation for all production and test
  modules.
- [ ] Build the distributable package and verify exact expanded and archive
  contents.
- [ ] Record live native-overlay input evidence for raw and unobstructed map
  events, public consumption, inherited pass-through, UI obstruction, and
  private-consumer suppression.
- [ ] Record live Lua-screen input evidence for the same supported contracts.
- [ ] Record installed-runtime evidence separately from source-checkout evidence.
- [ ] Record explicit reload evidence showing trampoline adoption, stale
  generation rejection, one runtime, one hook chain, and one pointer poller.
- [ ] Record cleanup evidence proving temporary subscriptions, test UI, hooks,
  and runtime state are removed or returned to their intended inert state.
- [ ] Do not claim a live behavior from unit, source, syntax, package, or static
  evidence alone.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] Focused, regression, full-unit, syntax, source, and package validations all
  pass and are recorded as distinct results.
- [ ] Native, Lua-screen, installed-runtime, reload, and cleanup evidence is
  recorded independently with terminal results.
- [ ] Any failed or unavailable evidence remains unchecked and is not presented
  as completion.

## Final acceptance

- [ ] `InputEventServiceProvider` follows the immutable namespace-bound
  acquisition pattern and shares one healthy process runtime.
- [ ] Public registration and handle operations enforce service, contract,
  namespace, generation, and identity boundaries.
- [ ] Every intercepted input table creates one immutable demand-aware snapshot
  before private arbitration, and all private consumers receive that snapshot
  with the unchanged read-only original keys table.
- [ ] Private consumers do not resample coordinates; private consumption
  suppresses both public channels and inherited handling.
- [ ] Every qualifying host-classified mouse-input table samples map position at
  most once and publishes at most one immutable raw event and one immutable
  semantic event with shared sequence, copied positions, and sorted mouse input.
- [ ] Coordinates are stored only as immutable `ScreenPosition` and
  `MapTilePosition` values, never as parallel axis fields.
- [ ] `MAP_CLICK` requires complete proof that no active UI target or blocker is
  in the way; pass-through UI does not obstruct, while targeted, blocking,
  unknown, and unsupported UI-resolution surfaces do.
- [ ] `RAW_CLICK` requires an exact map coordinate but does not claim that UI was
  absent; UI-resolution failure or known obstruction alone does not suppress it.
- [ ] Non-mouse, off-map, and private-Core-consumed input publishes neither
  public event.
- [ ] Semantic and raw subscriptions interleave deterministically by global
  registration order within interception and observation.
- [ ] The first valid public `CONSUME` stops later interceptors and inherited
  handling; failures and invalid returns are recorded and treated as `PASS`.
- [ ] Observers run after inherited handling or skipped delegation, use the
  pre-delegation snapshot, and cannot consume or alter inherited results.
- [ ] Callback ordering, registration mutation, removal, recursion, and failure
  containment are deterministic and covered.
- [ ] Native and Lua input seams preserve wrapper-chain, repair, reload, and
  owned-cleanup behavior.
- [ ] Tooltip uses shared demand-driven pointer samples while preserving tooltip
  semantics.
- [ ] Context menu uses shared intercepted snapshots while preserving menu
  semantics.
- [ ] UserPrompt retains prompt-first arbitration and does not leak owned input
  to public dispatch.
- [ ] Exactly one process hook and one pointer poller remain after migration, and
  map-coordinate polling does not occur without current map demand.
- [ ] Every proposal requirement is implemented or explicitly deferred with a
  documented rationale approved against the source proposal.
- [ ] Required source, unit, integration, package, live-runtime, reload, and
  cleanup evidence is complete and recorded separately.
